    # Handover @handover
    {
        Where this is as of 10 Oct 2026, after v0.4.0 was released and published
        cleanly to both registries.

        ## State @handover-state
        {
            - **v0.4.0 is released and live on both registries**: npm as
              \`@entropicwarrior/sdoc@0.4.0\`, over OIDC with a signed provenance
              statement, and the VS Code Marketplace as
              \`entropicwarrior-msenfin.vscode-sdoc\`. Both verified at the
              registry rather than from the workflow's exit code, and the
              published tarball install-tested
            - \`main\` and \`develop\` have identical trees, both at 0.4.0. The
              remote carries only those two branches and the tags. No drift
            - 1,101 tests green: 749 fast, 352 browser. The Python binding is
              37 of 38 — the wheel test needs \`setuptools\`, which Python 3.12+
              no longer bundles, and that failure is environmental
            - **The split release workflow is proven.** v0.4.0 was its first
              real run: npm finished while the Marketplace job was still going,
              which is exactly the coupling that cost v0.3.0 its npm publish
            - Open, in the order worth taking them: the colour-safety rule for
              the artifact export (a foreground colour whose background was
              dropped should not be carried — unwritten); the 31s a one-slide
              PDF takes, which is repeatable and unexplained and which the
              frame-rate flag does not touch; and the Chrome process-group kill
              in \`slide-geometry.js\`, where the mechanism is proven and the fix
              is not — see the traps below

        }

        ## What the v0.3.0 release cost, and why @handover-release
        {
            Read this before touching anything that publishes. Every item was
            confirmed against a real run, not inferred from the docs.

            - **One job meant one outage stopped both registries.** The Marketplace
              answered \`HTTP 503\`, the job stopped there, and the npm step later in
              the same job never ran — so a transient outage at one registry held
              back a package that had nothing to do with it. They are two jobs with
              no dependency now (PR #74)
            - **A \`release\` event runs the workflow stored at the tagged commit**,
              not the one on \`main\` or \`develop\`. So a fix to \`release.yml\` cannot
              rescue a release that is already tagged — re-publishing that release
              re-runs the old file. The fix applies from the next tag
            - **A trusted publisher listed by \`npm trust list\` can be dead.** The
              rescue path \`npm-publish.yml\` failed four times with \`E404\` while its
              connection was listed with the right repository, the right filename
              and both permissions. Revoking it and creating an identical one fixed
              it at once. Nothing in the listing told the dead one from the live one
            - **npm's \`E404\` on a publish is not about the package.** It is the
              registry's answer to an unauthenticated write: when the OIDC exchange
              fails, npm falls back to the placeholder token \`setup-node\` writes,
              and the 404 you see is that fallback failing. The real cause appears
              only under \`--loglevel verbose\`, which \`npm-publish.yml\` now carries
              permanently
            - **A publish that reports success may not be visible yet.** The PUT
              returns \`202 Accepted\` and npm processes it asynchronously — 0.3.0
              took about seven minutes to appear, 0.4.0 about thirty seconds, and
              the Marketplace five to eight minutes either time. \`npm view\` reads
              a local cache too, so query \`registry.npmjs.org\` directly before
              concluding that a publish failed and re-running one
            - **"Bypassed rule violations" is a success notice, not a failure.**
              Deleting a branch or pushing to \`main\` prints the rule it waived —
              \`- Cannot delete this branch\` — above the line that says what
              actually happened. Read to the \`- [deleted]\` or the ref update
              before concluding anything, and note git exits 0 either way

        }

        ## What a reader should not re-learn @handover-traps
        {
            - **The repo is public.** It had words in it tying back to a private
              deck — an element id naming a chip architecture, an internal repo
              name, another company's framing in a stats fixture. All scrubbed from
              the working tree and from this branch's history. Two commit messages
              on \`main\` still carry an internal repo name; that was a deliberate
              decision not to rewrite published history on the default branch
            - **A clean number from \`artifact-fidelity.js\` is not a correct slide.**
              It renders in Chrome, not the Slides viewer, and it measures geometry.
              Its own header says what it cannot see; read that before trusting it
            - **Every serious defect here produces a reassuring output, and the
              checks that miss them agree with themselves.** A guard over an empty
              corpus; a fixture that passed either way; a measurement whose two
              sides shared a flaw; a placement check whose loop had no body; an
              assertion that searched a whole slide for a property the
              \`<section>\` also sets, written inside the test meant to prove a
              cell carried it. The only reliable test is: **break it on purpose
              and watch the check fail**, one assertion at a time so none masks
              another. If it cannot be made to fail it is not a check
            - **When the corpus lacks the case, extend the fixture.** 885 tests
              passed over an exporter with four live defects because nothing in
              this repo had an inline-block inside a table cell, and nothing used
              \`text-indent\` at all. Adding the case to the theme the test builds
              is the version of this lesson that scales; "look at the rendered
              output" finds the defect but does not become a suite
            - **Reasoning from source loses to measuring, reliably.** Four
              diagnoses in one evening did not survive a computed value: a
              harvest-timeout blamed on image weight, a cover defect blamed on
              pseudo-element pinning, a \`:first-child\` that was matching all
              along, and a flex guard written to exclude the wrong initial value
              (\`0 1 auto\`, not \`0 0 auto\` — verified in the browser). Each was
              settled by one measurement
            - **Your own greps and normalisers will mislead you.** A \`sed\` that
              strips \`flex:none\` leaves the semicolon and reports every file as
              changed; \`grep\` patterns holding \`${...}\` or \`$\` get eaten by the
              shell or treated as anchors; BSD \`find -newermt\` fails silently.
              A normaliser that removes a thing is a parser written badly — parse
              the structure and compare the parse, and use \`grep -F\` for literals
            - **Headless Chrome needs \`--disable-frame-rate-limit\` here.** No
              display means no vsync, which means no frames and no
              \`requestAnimationFrame\`; the measuring script waits on one. The
              flag is in \`dumpDom\`, deliberately not in \`slide-pdf.js\` where it
              was measured and made no difference. Chrome also never exits on
              this machine while the DOM lands in seconds, so the harvest polls
              for its sentinel — anything that waits on process exit hangs
              forever, which is how two wrong timeout theories were reached
            - **The viewer differs from Chrome** in ways that have shipped broken
              slides four times. AGENTS.md lists what publishing has proved it will
              not carry. Probe anything load-bearing on a live artifact
            - **Both harvests are template literals.** Double every backslash and
              never write a backtick inside one, comments included

        }

    }

    # Current State @current-state
    {
        SDOC v0.6.1 is the current release. The parser, HTML renderer, slide renderer, slide export pipeline (PDF, PowerPoint / Google Slides, Claude Slides) and VS Code extension are stable and published to both npm and the VS Code Marketplace (auto-updates enabled). The format supports scope types, data blocks, comment scopes, table formulas, email auto-detection, collapsible scopes in all HTML output, and a full inline formatting suite. Issues are open on github/markup and github-linguist for GitHub rendering and language recognition.

        The spec is expected to evolve. The core principle of explicit brace scoping is stable. Everything else is open to change, with the constraint that existing documents must remain valid or be mechanically migrateable ([R10](requirements.sdoc#r10)).
    }

    # Completed @completed
    {
        The parser, renderer, and tooling are feature-complete for v0.2. See `specification.sdoc` for the full feature set. Key milestones:

        {[.]
            - v0.2.0 — scope types, data blocks, comment scopes, table width/alignment, key:value meta, line comments, K&R brace style, braceless scopes, implicit root scope
            - v0.2.4 — escaped pipes in table cells
            - v0.2.5 — auto-detected email addresses, file watcher for external preview writes
            - v0.2.6 — table formulas (=SUM, =AVG, =COUNT, arithmetic, A1 references, circular reference detection)
            - v0.2.7 — VS Code Marketplace publishing (entropicwarrior-msenfin.vscode-sdoc), progressive disclosure API documented
            - v0.2.8 — collapsible scopes and copy-to-clipboard in all standalone HTML output
            - v0.2.12 — citations (\`[@key]\` inline references, \`{[citations]\` definition blocks, auto-numbering by order of first appearance, back-links, validation)
            - v0.2.13 — column directives: per-column alignment (\`< > =\`) and number formatting (\`$\`, \`,.N\`, \`.N\`, \`%\`) in table directive rows
            - v0.2.19 — structured slide layouts, theme loading with \`theme.json\`, geometry harvest, PowerPoint / Google Slides export
            - v0.2.21 — optional slides, held back from the formats a deck is sent on in
            - v0.2.22 — drilldown navigation driven by position rather than a build-time class; independent navigation axes (left/right is the spine, up/down the column); deck images resolved against the \`.sdoc\` and embedded in every output format
            - v0.2.24 — the default design box became 1920x1080, the canvas a Claude Slides artifact is fixed at, with the print page following at 20 x 11.25in (the same geometry at 96 dpi). Nothing changes on screen, since a deck is scaled to the window whatever its box; the Claude Slides export now copies lengths across instead of scaling them. The theme's type scale was deliberately left in proportion rather than raised to that format's recommendation, which overflowed two example slides
            - v0.2.23 — the \`scatter\` layout; slide background images with placement, scale, flip and linear or radial fades; a Claude Slides artifact export with its own validator and a pull/diff that reads a collaborator's edits back; a deck-scoped stylesheet through the existing \`style-append:\` key; the presenter filmstrip; and two silent-corruption fixes — a scope left unclosed no longer parses to a different tree without saying so, and a theme's \`text-transform\` no longer turns "40 mW" into "40 MW"
            - v0.4.0 — signposts, the reserved scopes that describe a document rather than its subject: \`\@about\` and four new optional ones, \`\@not-about\`, \`\@related-resources\`, \`\@reading-guide\` and \`\@editing-guide\`. Each has a content model (prose, or a table with a fixed header) that \`validateSignposts\` checks, with \`\@not-about\` holding no links and \`\@about\` no section references; findings reach the VS Code Problems panel and \`build-doc.js --check\`. Exports keep only \`\@reading-guide\` by default; \`includeSignposts\` keeps all of them, \`includeAbout\` adds \`\@about\`, and \`signposts\` keeps or drops single sections (\`--include-signposts\`, \`--include-about\`, \`--signposts\` on the CLI). \`extractRelatedResources\` and \`extractReadingGuide\` read the two structured sections as data. Out-of-order signposts are a warning, fixed by the VS Code quick fix "Reorder signposts" or \`build-doc.js --fix\` (\`fixSignpostOrder\`), which refuses when moving the text would change the tree. The repo's own documents have not adopted the new sections; \`examples/example.sdoc\` shows all five
            - v0.5.0 — an \`\@id\` reference renders in HTML with its target's title as the link text (the title's inline formatting kept, any link or reference inside it flattened so no anchor nests in another), and the href unchanged. Resolution is \`validateRefs\`' own (\`collectRefTargets\`), so a reference never shows a title unless it validates. A target with no title, or an id that resolves to nothing, still shows \`\@id\`, and a broken reference keeps its warning. \`refLabels: "id"\` restores the old text. The slide and Notion renderers are unchanged: both print a reference as bare \`\@id\` text, not a link
            - v0.6.0 — standalone HTML follows the reader's dark mode, with the dark palette the VS Code preview uses, which now lives once in \`sdoc.js\` (\`DARK_STYLE\`) instead of in \`extension.js\`. The root element carries \`data-sdoc-theme\` (light or dark), set by a head script from \`prefers-color-scheme\` in the browser and from the editor's theme in the preview, and it is the one hook a stylesheet needs for both. sdoc's dark rules sit between the base stylesheet and the appended ones, so a rule there with the same selector wins. \`colorScheme\` (\`--color-scheme\` on \`build-doc\`) forces light or dark. Print stays light twice over: the dark rules are screen-only, and the attribute turns light on \`beforeprint\`; a PDF is built light outright, because Mermaid draws its diagram before printing. Measured in headless Chrome rather than inferred: the preview's computed colours are unchanged in light and dark, the browser's dark page matches the preview's exactly, and a forced-dark page prints light where both guards off prints dark. The document browser stays light, since its interface has no dark mode
            - v0.6.1 — the Claude Slides export emits a grid's cells in the order the grid placed them. The subset has \`grid-column\`/\`grid-row\` as \`span N\` only (format.md: "cells fill in source order"), so a cell placed with a line number cannot be told where to go; emitted in source order it took whatever cell was next. Found on a real deck (label, bar, value placed with \`grid-column\`, the value written first, exported as label, value, bar), and our own \`scatter\` had its x axis under the y gutter in every export, recorded as correct in the golden. The harvest measures each cell's row, column and span against the grid's resolved tracks (\`gridCell\`), seeing through \`display: contents\`; the emitter sorts by it, adds \`span N\`, fills an empty place with an empty cell, keeps a cell it would drop as an empty one, and warns (\`grid-overlap\`) when two cells share a track. An empty cell holding a place has not been checked on a live artifact
            - v0.6.1 — \`build-slides.js --no-notes\` (\`includeNotes: false\` on \`renderSlides\`) leaves every slide's \`\@notes\` out of the HTML, so out of every export too: the PowerPoint's speaker notes and the Claude Slides \`<aside>\` and outline descriptions are all read back from the page. Hidden notes were always in the file. And a PDF export that wrote nothing no longer reports success: Chrome neither creates the output folder nor fails when it cannot write, so \`chromePdf\` now makes the folder and checks that this run wrote the file, which covers \`build-doc\` and the VS Code export as well
            - v0.6.1 — the slide HTML build carries no CSS comments: the theme's, the deck's \`style-append:\` and each \`--css\` sheet are stripped as one (\`stripCssComments\`), keeping \`/*!\` licence notices. Found on a deck built with \`--no-optional --no-notes\` to be emailed: 264 comments, 149,000 characters of design reasoning naming people and decisions, all legible in the page's source. Always, not behind a flag, since a flag is what was forgotten. The stripper respects strings and unquoted \`url()\`, and puts a space only where a comment separated two words (\`1px/**/2px\`), since one elsewhere changes a selector (\`a/**/.b\` is \`a.b\`). Every golden held: no example deck renders differently. The Claude Slides export never carried a stylesheet's comments
        }

